      "section_bonus": 0.15,
      "adjusted_retrieval_score": 0.43830260038375857,
      "adjusted_retrieval_score_norm": 0.7969565923754627,
      "rerank_score": -5.99891471862793,
      "rerank_score_norm": 0.2991335827567359,
      "final_score": 0.6227185390089083,
      "text": "This meta-­ analysis of studies originating from the United States and Canada on adult and pediatric populations showed that opioids are not more effective than nonopioids (mostly NSAIDs) in managing acute pain after ED discharge. However, a sensitivity analysis showed that opioids other than codeine provided better pain relief than nonopioids. Additionally, opioids were associated in all studies with a higher rate of adverse events and in one study with higher use of rescue medications. 41 While we planned to include RCTs on all types of acute outpatient pain management after ED discharge, we found only studies including patients with musculoskeletal injuries. We did not observe a significant difference in results between adult and pediatric populations. FIGURE 2 Forest plot of opioid efficacy after ED discharge. FIGURE 3 Forest plot of adverse events after ED discharge. | FIGURE 4 Forest plot of opioid efficacy for codeine versus other opioids.",
